cture context lists\n\"Milvus/vector providers for RAG workflows\" — verified in\nrhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md). No scope duplication across sibling epics: A-E006\nand B-E002 both wire Llama Stack provider consumption, but they live in\nmutually exclusive conditional branches, not co-existing siblings; within each\nbranch the operator (A-E005), Dashboard (A-E007), and Llama Stack (A-E006)\nsurfaces are cleanly disjoint. Work-product types are not mixed: both docs\nepics (A-E008, B-E004) are pure docs-authoring, separate from code epics. No\nepic appears oversized; B-E002 is the largest (registration + validation +\nconsumption + sanctioned-type definition) but is one logical change on one\ncomponent with a shared contract, plausibly a single sprint.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (managed vs external posture)\ndetermines the existence of all downstream epics — it forks the plan, exactly\nthe misclassification test's positive case. All ten branch epics are\nImplementations producing concrete artifacts (repository, clearance record,\nbuild pipeline, operator capability, provider wiring, UI, published docs).\nGating metadata is complete and correct on every branch epic: gated_by is set,\ngate_failure_impact has action=remove with a populated fallback_approach\ndescribing the counterpart branch, and the gated_by target (E001) appears in\neach epic's direct dependencies. Every epic listing the Investigation in\ndependencies has gated_by set. E001 itself correctly has gated_by null. The\ngates are true gates, not scheduling dependencies — the outcome removes the\nentire branch.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001 carries investigation_signals\n(ai_signals null); all Implementation epics carry ai_signals\n(investigation_signals null); no cross-type contamination. All 11 ai-signals\nrationale files exist and their values match frontmatter exactly. E001's\nsignals are consistent with the rubric: question_specificity +1 (concrete,\nenumerable question with named criteria), source_accessibility 0 (the answer\nis a pending product decision — correctly not marked 1), human_judgment_required\n-2 (stakeholder sign-off decision). Signal values engage honestly with the\narchitecture context: A-E005 scores repo_access 0 and architecture_claims -1\nbecause the Platform Operator has no architecture context file (verified —\nonly llama-stack.md and dashboard.md exist), matching the health warning;\nA-E006/A-E007/B-E002/B-E003 score architecture_claims +1 backed by verifiable\ncontext (provider-plugin model, CR-based Dashboard integration,\nOdhDashboardConfig feature flags). B-E002's open_questions +1 is justified: the\nundefined supported-store breadth is captured as an acceptance criterion and\ndoes not change the epic's scope or existence.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy. Rule-\nmandated ACs verified on every applicable epic: A-E004 has the Konflux\nbuild-pipeline-green AC; A-E005 carries both the pinned-digest availability AC\nand a platform build-green AC; both docs epics (A-E008, B-E004) have the\ntechnical-review AC; both Dashboard epics (A-E007, B-E003) have feature-flag\nACs consistent with the Dashboard's conventions; A-E005 covers lifecycle\n(upgrade/scale/removal) and rollback behavior for the managed service. No epic\nbody references sibling epics by bare draft ID — grepped all bodies; only full\nepic IDs appear, and only in frontmatter.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is covered: the two-camp problem statement motivates both\nbranch variants of HLR-2; all HLRs map in both branches; the Out of Scope\nconstraints are respected (no both-postures epic, no embedding-model work).\nThe conditional branches cover exactly the two bounded outcomes named in the\nstrategy's open question, and the strategy's evaluation criteria\n(supportability cost,